Production readiness review - 2026-08-22

The application is NOT ready for production until the remaining Critical items below are resolved.

Critical

1. PARTIALLY FIXED: fail fast before listening when required configuration is missing.
   - ACCESS_TOKEN_SECRET, REFRESH_TOKEN_SECRET, DATABASE_URL, Twilio variables, and FRONTEND_ORIGIN are now checked at startup.
   - server.js still does not await the MongoDB connection before app.listen().
   - A database failure can still leave the process listening while requests fail.
   - Start listening only after MongoDB connects and terminate startup on connection failure.

2. PARTIALLY FIXED: make production origin and cookie settings environment-based.
   - CORS now uses FRONTEND_ORIGIN instead of a hardcoded localhost origin.
   - The refresh cookie is still hardcoded secure: true, which requires HTTPS and may silently fail outside HTTPS.
   - Use an explicit production allowlist, environment-based secure cookie configuration, and a documented same-site/HTTPS deployment policy. Never use a permissive wildcard with credentials.

3. RESOLVED FOR THIS REPOSITORY: verify that .env is never committed or exposed.
   - .env is listed in .gitignore and you confirmed it will not be committed.
   - Still confirm that it has never been committed or copied into deployment artifacts; rotate credentials if it was ever exposed.
   - Supply secrets through the deployment secret manager/environment.

High

4. done

5. Add security and request hardening.
   - Add security headers (for example Helmet).
   - Set an explicit JSON body-size limit instead of the unlimited default.
   - Configure reverse-proxy trust and rate-limit storage appropriately for multiple production instances.

6. Add complete appointment validation and business constraints.
   - Enforce length and format limits for fullName, phone, date, time, and details at the schema/request boundary.
   - Reject invalid or past appointment dates and invalid time values.
   - Prevent double booking with a database-backed uniqueness rule for the appointment slot, and return 409 on conflicts.
   - Apply phone validation on PATCH as well as POST.

7. Make logout and refresh-token session handling explicit.
   - Confirm refresh token rotation/reuse detection is required for the threat model.
   - Expire and remove old sessions, enforce expiresAt when refreshing, and limit the number of active sessions per user.
   - Consider revoking refresh sessions on password/security changes.

8. Add automated tests before release.
   - Cover authentication failures, admin authorization, malformed IDs, missing appointments, validation errors, duplicate slots, malformed JSON, rate limits, and generic 500 responses.
   - Assert that no response contains stack traces, database messages, Twilio messages, JWT secrets, or other internal details.

Medium

9. Improve startup and shutdown reliability.
   - Handle MongoDB connection failure as a startup failure.
   - Add graceful shutdown for the HTTP server and MongoDB connection on SIGTERM/SIGINT.
   - Add health/readiness endpoints that reflect database availability.

10. Add production observability.
    - Use structured logs with request IDs and error context, while redacting tokens, cookies, passwords, phone numbers, and other personal data.
    - Add monitoring/alerts for startup failures, database failures, authentication abuse, and elevated 5xx responses.

11. Define production package/deployment configuration.
    - Add a production start script and document required environment variables.
    - Pin and regularly audit dependencies; run npm audit in CI.
    - Run the process under a non-root user and place it behind HTTPS/reverse proxy with appropriate timeout limits.

Resolved in code

- Admin-only access is present on appointment list/detail/update/delete routes.
- Login is rate-limited.
- Refresh-token lookup checks that the session still exists in MongoDB.
- Raw caught error messages are no longer returned to clients.
- Unknown routes return 404 and invalid/missing appointment IDs return 400/404.
- Appointment creation returns 201 and update validation is enabled.
- Appointment update writes details, matching the schema field.
- Twilio validation failures no longer return provider error details.
- Fix the JWT user name field.